= Accountability and the Armed Forces

**14 August 1985 — Accomarca, Ayacucho**

Soldiers killed civilians in Accomarca during the internal armed conflict. The incident becomes an early test of the new administration's ability to hold security forces accountable.

The Historical Truth and Reconciliation Commission later documented the massacre as part of its investigation into Peru's political violence.

Your government must decide how to respond.

- @apra_accomarca_investigation: Order an independent investigation.
- @apra_accomarca_command: Protect the military command.

@apra_accomarca_investigation
title: Order an Independent Investigation
on-arrival: {!
Q.legitimacy = Math.min(100, Q.legitimacy + 5);
Q.military_confidence = Math.max(0, Q.military_confidence - 4);
Q.govt_card_available = 0;
!}

You demand an investigation and insist that civilian authorities must be able to examine allegations of wrongdoing. This strengthens the government's accountability commitments but risks conflict with the military command.

- @main: Return to the presidential desk

@apra_accomarca_command
title: Protect the Military Command
on-arrival: {!
Q.military_confidence = Math.min(100, Q.military_confidence + 3);
Q.legitimacy = Math.max(0, Q.legitimacy - 5);
Q.govt_card_available = 0;
!}

You prioritise unity within the security establishment. The decision may reduce immediate friction with military leaders, but it damages the government's credibility on accountability.

- @main: Return to the presidential desk
